Negative control — the manager's UPDATE limb (AC-23, parity row L3).

Amputates the `update` service in `useClientAddressManagerServices` — the limb
`processing.updating` invokes when the context already carries an id — so an
edit can never reach the diffing `update`, and therefore never reaches the wire
as a PUT.

Should flip RED — __tests__/client-address.manager-save.int.test.ts:
  - "AC-23 puts ONLY the changed field and does NOT re-send the unchanged
     country"                   (no PUT is captured; the save rejects)
  - "AC-23 sends nothing at all beyond the fields that actually moved"
  - "AC-22/AC-23 puts the chosen address type on the wire when the type is what
     changed"
Collateral RED (both drive their read-back THROUGH an edit, so the amputated
PUT takes them with it; neither is this control's declared subject):
  - "AC-15 refetches the collection off the save, with no consumer-side refresh"
  - the AC-30 pinned-client save read-back

What this does NOT cover — AC-24. Create and update are two distinct limbs of
the same services adapter: `add` routes to `service.ensure` (find-or-create ->
POST), `update` routes to the diffing `update` (-> PUT). This mutant amputates
the update limb only, and BOTH AC-24 tests were measured GREEN under it — the
create path survives it untouched. AC-24's control is the sibling mutant
`client-address.manager-create-amputation.must-fail.patch`. One mutant per
capability, so a red localises to the limb that broke.

